A||||11||MooCow||How to tell when in need for shocks??||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||08-15-2001||11:26 PM||||I think my shocks are bad on my car now.  It seems to be geting more bumber than when I first had it.  Will shocks make the ride more smoother or do I need other things too??  Also how can you tell when shocks are bad??<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_7.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Songman||08-15-2001||11:38 PM||||The old way is to stand at one corner of the car and bounce the car three times. If it bounces more than once after you stop then it needs a shock on that corner. Repeat on all four corners. Not sure if that will work on a Fiero or not.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||08-15-2001||11:40 PM||||push down on the side where you think the shock is bad, try to get the car to bounce... if it keeps bouncing after you stoped pushing on it - it needs shocks, if it stops bouncing right away then they are good||||reg||1
Z||000003||KRMFiero||08-15-2001||11:41 PM||||I was beat to it! I need to type faster, i am almost postive it will work on Fieros' i dont know about the back though because the Fiero has stuts - should work though<P>remember to replace shocks/stuts in pairs<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000004||btoth||08-15-2001||11:43 PM||||How do you know when you need springs?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>When I accelerate I can feel the front lift, and when I brake the front dives down. (At least from what I feel in the car.) Also the front sits lower than the rear.  Is that a good indication?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000005||fieronv||08-16-2001||12:09 AM||||btoth <BR>My car has lifted and dived since I got it new. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>But if you are setting noticeably lower then your shocks maybe going bad. Struts are harder to check with the bounce test but keep an eye out for a saggy rear end.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||KRMFiero||08-16-2001||12:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>How do you know when you need springs?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>When I accelerate I can feel the front lift, and when I brake the front dives down. (At least from what I feel in the car.) Also the front sits lower than the rear.  Is that a good indication?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>that is the indication that u have a pre 88 Fiero, engineered shitty, both mine do that - whats the fix?:<BR>get stiffer springs<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||MooCow||08-16-2001||12:37 AM||||So how much are shocks, and struts?? Also are they hard to install??  Do I have to get it re-aligned after they are installed??  As you can tell I have never messed with anything as far as suspension goes but brakes.<P>Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000008||KRMFiero||08-16-2001||12:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MooCow:<BR><B>So how much are shocks, and struts?? Also are they hard to install??  Do I have to get it re-aligned after they are installed??  As you can tell I have never messed with anything as far as suspension goes but brakes.<P>Thanks Ken</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>For Cheap Monroe shocks at any autoparts store should only be say 20-30$ each  and they are very simple to install, two bolts and u dont have to realign anything<P>With the stuts they wil probley be closer to 50$ each but you want to pay someone to install them, u can get hert. <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A>  sells good shock/stuts, prices arent to bad, i want to get KYB's as soon as i get enough $$$<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tigger||08-16-2001||08:11 AM||||Believe another good indicator is to check for oil seeping from the shocks and struts.<P>I was following a car w/bad shocks the other day and the front end was bouncing up and down like a surf board even on a fairly smooth highway.  It was kinda funny but scary.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||ferg||08-16-2001||08:50 AM||||moocow- I just finished doing my shocks and struts on my 86- very very easy to do-<P>cost- KYB front shocks- approx- $25/ea<BR>      KYB rear struts- approx - $60/ea<P>I purchased them from the Tire Rack. Had no problems with them<P>you can see pics of the procedure here <A HREF="http://pages.prodigy.net/fergusor/myupgradepics.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://pages.prodigy.net/fergusor/myupgradepics.htm</A> <P>You will need an alignment once you're done. I'm having trouble in this area right now- I think it's a simple fix, and I'm just dealing with idiots.<P>for the shocks- unbolt the old ones, replace with new- yer done.<P>for the struts- unbolt the brake line, there are 2 BIG bolts that hold the spindle to the strut body- unbolt those- in the engine bay, you'll see 3 nuts, take all that apart- the strut just drops out- when it's out of the car- you need to compress the spring w/ a spring compressor- (I bought the one in the pics for about $40) once you compress the springs, you unbolt all the stuff on the top- take out the old strut, and replace, put it all back together in reverse order- have it aligned- job done.<P>[This message has been edited by ferg (edited 08-16-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by ferg (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||stsmithgt||08-16-2001||09:54 AM||||If you don't know how old your shocks are, you probably need new ones.  Mine were probably the original 1986's.  It was like comparing night and day on the difference in ride and handling when I had my shocks and struts changed and I only went with Monroe roadhandlers. (I think that's what they were called)  They must have sucked when I bought the car.  I would have changed them years ago if I knew how much the handling would improve.  The control over uneveness and dips in the road became so much easier to drive over.  I can feel a big difference just going up a driveway.  I don't thrash my car so I didn't think I needed to spend so much on Koni's.  The car is a money pit just keeping it <I>almost</I> stock. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
